p='peter_gay';s='gay_interview_1997'
P(p,
'A historian of European intellectual and cultural life who used psychoanalysis while resisting reduction of historical events to inner experience. In his 1997 interview, Gay describes Freud as an indispensable resource for studying interaction between psychological processes and external circumstances. The French Revolution, in his example, still requires attention to the state’s finances, the Crown’s prestige and social conflict. He recalls Neumann’s reading group as an early stimulus and later professional training as a way to acquire both understanding and critical distance. His criticism of reductionist psychohistory qualifies his placement in that field. The Enlightenment, Weimar Culture and The Bourgeois Experience mark distinct historical projects. In a later publisher excerpt he also challenges a unified account of romanticism as the Enlightenment’s opposite. These positions support a particular historian’s psychoanalytic practice, rather than a claim that psychoanalysis supplies a complete explanation of history.',
'Fresh extended reading of the posted 1997 interview’s professional answers: definition of psychohistory, Freud/Neumann stimulus, dual training, scholarly examples and disagreements. Personal/family material excluded. Posted text is an excerpt from September 1997, not a full recorded interview. Earlier authorized romanticism excerpt retained; complete books and Freud/Neumann originals unread. Interviewers’ prompts are not treated as Gay’s acknowledgments.',
existing=[s],
edges=[E(p,'freud','sigmund_freud',p,'Gay uses Freud as a resource for studying the interaction of inner processes and historical circumstances.',s,'Primary answers to definition of psychohistory and its origins: external constraints, French Revolution example, Neumann reading group. Conceptual resource, not a claim that Freud himself wrote adequate historical studies.'),E(p,'reductionism',p,'psychohistory','Gay criticizes psychohistory when it assigns excessive causal weight to inner experience and neglects external historical circumstances.',s,'Primary opening professional answer distinguishes his psychoanalytically informed practice from reductionism, with subsequent historical example. Criticism qualifies the retained contribution relationship.',kind='critique')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Fresh extended professional answers from definition through scholarly examples; excludes personal/family material')],holds=['Neumann, Erikson and Demos have no individual endpoints. No blanket rejection of psychoanalysis or independent endorsement of Gay’s clinical claims.'])

p='paul_thompson';s='tq234_thompson_worcman';t='tq234_thompson_taylor2021'
P(p,
'A social and oral historian concerned with how interviews change both historical evidence and research practice. His retrospective interview credits Townsend’s socially engaged research and George Ewart Evans’s listening methods, and recalls resisting Hobsbawm’s request to remove oral-history material from The Edwardians. Thompson distinguishes his early emphasis on reliable recollection from later attention to subjectivity, silence and memory associated with Passerini and Portelli. His community-history discussion also warns that participation can exclude less confident people and need not produce progressive politics. Becky Taylor’s reassessment credits the fishing project’s attention to change and its preservation of sources, while questioning interview schedules, uneven transcription and the insufficiently examined category of fisherman. Her reuse demonstrates how an archive can support questions its makers did not fully pursue. Living the Fishing was collaborative work with Tony Wailey and Trevor Lummis, not Thompson’s solitary project.',
'Fresh primary UK Data Archive thematic highlights of Karen Worcman’s 1996/2002 interview: selected printed pp. 5–8,11–13, professional-method paragraphs only. Archive cover identifies Thompson correctly despite misleading running heading; excerpt, not full transcript. The Portelli forename is defective in the transcription and is not reproduced. Taylor’s 2021 article selected opening, section I methodological paragraphs, category criticism and conclusion read; community testimony/identities not retained. Earlier community excerpt’s reader pagination remains distinct from original book pages.',
sources=[S(s,'Paul Thompson, interview with Karen Worcman (1996, 2002), UK Data Archive thematic highlights, June 2017, SN6226.','https://discover.ukdataservice.ac.uk/assets/qualibank/6226themext029a.pdf','Repository interview excerpt: selected professional-method paragraphs on printed pp. 5–8,11–13 read. Townsend, Hobsbawm, listening, reliability and later subjectivity. Cover identifies Thompson despite running-head defect; no full-transcript claim, narrator quotation or community/family content retained.'),S(t,'Becky Taylor, “Who was Ever ‘a Fisherman’? Revisiting Paul Thompson’s Living the Fishing,” Twentieth Century British History 32:3 (2021): 416–440, DOI 10.1093/tcbh/hwab008.','https://doi.org/10.1093/tcbh/hwab008','Publisher text: selected opening, section I methodological paragraphs, category criticism and conclusion read. Credits collaborative research/archiving while questioning schedules, transcription and fixed worker category. No original interview recordings read and no narrator testimony or community details retained.')],
edges=[E(p,'hobsbawm','eric_hobsbawm',p,'Hobsbawm’s commission helped generate The Edwardians, although Thompson recalls disagreement over its oral-history material.',s,'Thematic highlights printed pp. 6–8, sections 8–9, Thompson’s retrospective account of commission and refusal to remove interviews. Hobsbawm’s own account unread; does not credit him with promoting this method.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'oral','Develops interview-based historical inquiry while reconsidering the relation between reliable recollection, subjectivity and the purposes of participation.',['thompson_community_primary',s],'Existing community excerpt plus thematic highlights pp. 11–13, sections 13–15. Early and later editions distinguished; narratives not reproduced.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected printed pp. 5–8,11–13, professional-method paragraphs'),dict(source_id=t,locator='Selected opening; section I methods/category criticism; conclusion, with nn8,17–22,25–27')],holds=['2020 interview body unread after access check. Townsend, Hoskins, George Ewart Evans, Passerini and Taylor have no individual endpoints. No oral material is quoted or reproduced.'])

p='charles_e_rosenberg';s='tq234_rosenberg_mantovani2016'
P(p,
'A historian of medicine and science who connects disease categories, institutions and changing social relations. In his interview with Rafael Mantovani, Rosenberg places social medicine within a tradition that treats medical ideas and access to care as embedded in society. The interviewer identifies Ackerknecht and Rosen as formative teachers; Rosenberg’s own answers describe the tradition’s social commitments without reducing it to a single political orthodoxy. He also questions a tidy genealogy in which translated theorists alone cause changes in historical research, emphasizing diffuse criticism and eclectic practice. His discussion of diagnosis distinguishes classifications that organize research and care from explanations of mechanisms. The Cholera Years, The Care of Strangers and Explaining Epidemics mark different parts of this programme. The earlier epidemic chapter headnote supplies a more limited primary witness; its whole argument is not inferred from that opening.',
'Fresh selected interview reading: Mantovani’s introduction p. 212; Rosenberg’s professional historiographical answers pp. 213–216 and methodological discussion of diagnosis pp. 218–219. Conducted November 2013, published 2016, DOI 10.1590/S0104-59702016000100013. Policy discussion omitted from retained claims; no current clinical or policy guidance. Teacher attribution is the interviewer’s account, not silently converted to Rosenberg’s personal testimony. Complete books and named theorists’ originals unread.',
sources=[S(s,'Rafael Mantovani, interview with Charles Rosenberg, História, Ciências, Saúde – Manguinhos 23:1 (2016): 211–219, DOI 10.1590/S0104-59702016000100013.','https://doi.org/10.1590/S0104-59702016000100013','Original journal/SciELO text: selected p. 212 introduction, professional answers pp. 213–216 and diagnosis-method discussion pp. 218–219 read. Interview conducted November 2013. Introduction and answers distinguished; full books and health-policy assertions not independently checked.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'medicalhistory','Examines disease and diagnosis through the reciprocal relations of medical categories, institutions and social circumstances.',s,'Primary professional answers pp. 215–216,218–219: social-medicine tradition and classifications that organize practice without necessarily explaining mechanisms.'),R(p,2,p,'science','Questions simple theoretical genealogies and treats the authority and categories of medicine as historical problems.',s,'Primary historiographical answers pp. 213–215 and diagnosis discussion pp. 218–219. Does not infer a personal Foucault/Gramsci influence from the interviewer’s question.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected p. 212 introduction; professional answers pp. 213–216; diagnosis-method discussion pp. 218–219')],holds=['Ackerknecht, Rosen and Sigerist have no individual endpoints. No personal Foucault/Gramsci arrow based on a prompted question. Full epidemic chapter and books unread.'])

p='robert_bartlett';s='tq234_bartlett_race_extended2001'
P(p,
'A medieval historian of conquest, institutions and historical categories. His 2001 article treats both race and ethnicity as social constructions and warns against using present administrative labels as ready-made historical explanations. It then compares medieval terminology and argues for the importance of culture, law, language and religion in making identities. Bartlett’s point is not simply that medieval writers lacked descent thinking: he examines genealogical idioms while emphasizing changeable identifications. His discussion of nationhood also separates collective identity from an automatic demand for sovereign statehood. Geraldine Heng later questions the sharpness of his distinction between medieval cultural difference and biological racism. That criticism remains alongside his argument, rather than turning either position into a consensus. The Making of Europe, Trial by Fire and Water and England under the Norman and Angevin Kings locate a wider programme whose complete monographs remain unread here.',
'Fresh primary article reading expanded from opening pp. 39–40 to selected pp. 39–43,45–47,52–53; authorized St Andrews repository returned 200 after robots allowed access. Terminological argument and conceptual conclusions only retained, not quoted historical narratives. Heng’s previously checked selected critique remains a separate 2018 witness. Article pp. 48–51,54–56 and complete monographs not claimed read; cited medieval originals not independently collated. Omi/Winant affiliation remains rejected for lack of the specified theoretical connection.',
sources=[S(s,'Robert Bartlett, “Medieval and Modern Concepts of Race and Ethnicity,” Journal of Medieval and Early Modern Studies 31:1 (2001): 39–56, extended selected reading, DOI 10.1215/10829636-31-1-39.','https://doi.org/10.1215/10829636-31-1-39','Authorized St Andrews repository copy, selected pp. 39–43,45–47,52–53 read: both race/ethnicity constructed, terminology, culture/descent distinction, political sovereignty. Not full article or original medieval source reading. Later Heng criticism remains separately attributed.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'intellectualhistory','Historicizes racial and ethnic categories through their changing meanings and the political claims made with them.',s,'Primary selected pp. 39–43,45–47,52–53. Explicitly both categories constructed; does not equate construction with social insignificance.')],
edges=[E(p,'nationhood',p,'nationalismstudies','Distinguishes medieval collective identifications from an automatic requirement that political sovereignty follow linguistic or cultural boundaries.',s,'Primary pp. 52–53, comparison of political and ethnic arrangements. A bounded conceptual contribution, not endorsement of modern nationalist continuity.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected primary pp. 39–43,45–47,52–53'),dict(source_id='tc234_bartlett_heng',locator='Earlier selected 2018 critique pp. 586–593',fresh=False)],holds=['Heng and the modern authors named in the selected article lack individual endpoints. No reading of complete medieval syntheses, and no inferred Omi/Winant affiliation.'])

p='stephen_greenblatt';s='tq234_greenblatt_monfasani2012';c='tq234_greenblatt_culture1995';i='tc234_greenblatt_interview'
P(p,
'A literary theorist associated with New Historicism who studies literary form within historical relations. His retrospective account credits Raymond Williams with helping him connect Marxist literary criticism to the world in which works are made. He also values the formalist skills acquired in his earlier training, even as he challenged its exclusion of historical circumstances. In “Culture,” constraint and mobility depend on one another: improvisation and exchange help constitute cultural boundaries. This is more specific than treating context as a static background. His historical narratives also invite criticism. John Monfasani challenges The Swerve’s opposition between medieval darkness and Renaissance modernity and the evidence for Lucretius’s transformative influence; Greenblatt’s response explicitly defends the importance of the Renaissance and atomism. That dispute, alongside Maza’s disciplinary questions, qualifies the relationship between his literary practice and historians’ cultural history.',
'Fresh extended 2014 interview professional answers on Williams, formalist training and historical interpretation read; page dates the interview 3 June, correcting the draft source title’s 5 June. Authorized university teaching excerpts of “Culture,” second edition (1995), pp. 225,229 read, not entire essay. Monfasani’s July 2012 review and Greenblatt’s short posted response read; underlying Swerve and cited historical sources unread. New Historicism is not automatically rostered as the atlas’s linguistic-turn programme.',
sources=[S(c,'Stephen Greenblatt, “Culture,” Critical Terms for Literary Study, second edition (1995), teaching excerpts from pp. 225 and229.','[excluded route removed at Claude verification 2026-10-31]','University teaching share of selected essay passages, constraint/mobility and improvisation read. Original essay first published 1990; this is a 1995 edition excerpt, not the full essay or book.'),S(s,'John Monfasani, review of The Swerve, Reviews in History no. 1283 (July 2012), with Stephen Greenblatt’s response.','https://reviews.history.ac.uk/review/1283/','Original review and short author response read. Criticism of Renaissance rupture and influence evidence; author defends Renaissance significance and atomism. Underlying book and cited originals unread. UK and US subtitles differ; neither is backdated to another work.')],
existing=[i],
edges=[E(p,'williams','raymond_williams',p,'Greenblatt credits Williams’s Marxist literary criticism with helping him connect art to the historical worlds in which it operates.',i,'Fresh interview answers to Cambridge influences and his rebellion against New Criticism, together with his continuing appreciation of formal analysis. Specific acknowledged influence, not exclusive theoretical allegiance.',kind='influence')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'culture','Studies cultural boundaries through the interaction of constraint, improvisation, exchange and the historical situations of literary works.',c,'Teaching excerpts pp. 225,229, and fresh interview methodological answers. Does not collapse literary criticism into archival historical practice.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=i,locator='Extended professional answers on Cambridge influences, New Criticism and continued formalist practice'),dict(source_id=c,locator='Teaching excerpts pp. 225,229'),dict(source_id=s,locator='Complete posted review and short author response')],holds=['Monfasani, Maza and Burckhardt have no individual endpoints. Full books unread. The author’s defence is retained without adopting the disputed influence thesis as established history.'])
